<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      App Group shared container, paired with the same entitlement on
      ShareExtension/LlamasCookbookShareExtension.entitlements. Lets
      the share extension and the main app exchange `.llamarecipe`
      files via Sources/Shared/SharedContainer.swift's `share-inbox/`
      directory. See Share-Extension-Plan.md §3 + §7.

      Identifier MUST match SharedContainer.appGroupID and the App
      Group registered in Apple Developer Portal — three places, one
      string, no drift.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>group.com.llamascookbook.app</string>
    </array>
    <!--
      Sign in with Apple — identity primitive for cloud recipe delivery.
      See Implementing-User-Sign-In.md §3 + §10. Entitlement must be
      enabled on the App ID in Apple Developer Portal AND the
      provisioning profile regenerated after enabling it; the
      capability is baked into the profile at issue time, so a stale
      profile silently strips the entitlement at sign and the runtime
      `ASAuthorizationController` call fails with error 1000
      ("not handled").
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
    <array>
        <string>Default</string>
    </array>
    <!--
      iCloud / CloudKit — recipe-share permalink hosting. Sender
      uploads the share envelope (with photos as CKAsset) to the
      public DB, gets back a random 12-char record ID, mints
      `https://llamascookbook.pages.dev/r/<id>` (HTTPS Universal
      Link, AASA-routed back into the app) and ships that to the
      recipient. Recipient fetches by record ID; no Llamas
      Cookbook account required on the receive side, only a
      system iCloud account (which iPhone users effectively
      always have).

      Same portal+profile dance as Sign in with Apple above:
      capability enabled on the App ID in Apple Developer Portal,
      container `iCloud.com.llamascookbook.app` created, and the
      main app's provisioning profile regenerated after the
      capability is added — the entitlement is baked into the
      profile at issue time, so a stale profile fails sign with
      "Provisioning profile doesn't include the
      com.apple.developer.icloud-services entitlement." See
      Implementing-User-Sign-In.md §10.

      Container identifier MUST match `CloudKitService.containerID`
      in Sources/Lib/CloudKitService.swift — three places, one
      string, no drift (here, the Swift constant, and the App ID
      Capabilities config in the portal).
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
    <array>
        <string>iCloud.com.llamascookbook.app</string>
    </array>
    <key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
    <array>
        <string>CloudKit</string>
    </array>
    <!--
      Associated Domains — Universal Links for the recipe-share
      flow. Sender mints `https://llamascookbook.pages.dev/r/<id>`,
      Messages on the recipient renders the rich link preview by
      scraping our Cloudflare Pages function, and tapping the
      bubble opens directly into the app via this entitlement.

      Same portal+profile dance as iCloud / Sign in with Apple:
      capability enabled on the App ID in Apple Developer Portal,
      domains added to the capability config, and the main app's
      provisioning profile regenerated after — the entitlement is
      baked into the profile at issue time, so a stale profile
      silently strips the entitlement at sign and Universal Links
      open Safari instead of the app.

      The receiving side (Cloudflare Pages) hosts the matching
      `apple-app-site-association` JSON at
      `https://llamascookbook.pages.dev/.well-known/apple-app-site-association`,
      declaring our team+bundle ID against the `/r/*` path.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
    <array>
        <string>applinks:llamascookbook.pages.dev</string>
    </array>
    <!--
      APS (Apple Push Service) environment — required for
      CloudKit's CKQuerySubscription pushes to deliver to this
      app. Slice 6 of implement-social.md registers two
      subscriptions (friendship-events, recipe-import-events) so
      friend-request and import-audit events surface in near-
      real-time without waiting for a foreground refresh.

      `development` is the right value for non-production builds;
      Apple Developer Portal automatically substitutes
      `production` at provisioning time when the app is signed
      with a distribution profile. The string is literal-only
      here — we don't conditionalize per-build.

      Same portal+profile dance as the other capabilities in this
      file: Push Notifications capability enabled on the App ID
      in Apple Developer Portal AND the main app's provisioning
      profile regenerated after — the entitlement is baked into
      the profile at issue time, so a stale profile silently
      strips the entitlement at sign and CloudKit pushes never
      reach the device.

      No new GitHub Secret is needed for delivery — CloudKit
      handles APNs routing internally via the iCloud container
      that's already configured. (Pure APNs apps would need a
      `.p8` auth key uploaded to the portal, but CloudKit-mediated
      pushes pass through the iCloud trust without the key.)

      User-visible alert permissions are unrelated — those
      live behind `UNUserNotificationCenter.requestAuthorization`,
      which the cooking-timer feature already requests on first
      launch. Slice 6 uses silent pushes only
      (`shouldSendContentAvailable = true`, no alertBody), so
      the existing permission grant suffices.
    -->
    <key>aps-environment</key>
    <string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
